From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: TF_GLM_OVN_KEEP_SEQS, a released stream's sequence stays for the next request on its slot

Ours (lane RequestStartCost, 2026-11-14); nothing ported. Engine.release (cuda_engine.zig) frees every released
slot's sequence but slot 0's, so the next request on that slot pays Engine.bind's State.initFormat again: a cuMemAlloc
of the whole window's arena and a synchronous zero fill of it (cuda_state.zig arena(): DeviceBuffer.alloc, then
fill8(0, null)), and its release pays a drain and a cuMemFree. Rank 0 binds the slot before its begin step reaches
the other ranks (cuda_lanes.zig opening() -> seat() -> Leader.bind, then Leader.beginOn sends the step; cuda_follow.zig
follow() binds on receipt), so their allocation and fill run after rank 0's, and rank 0 waits for them in the chunk
gate's first all-reduce (cuda_engine_gate.zig shared(), the ncclAllReduce f64 that nsys names
ncclDevKernel_AllReduce_Sum_f64_TREE_LL). All of it is proportional to --context, none of it to the request.

The knob, a .glm row of platform/environment.zig read through cuda/ovn.zig: TF_GLM_OVN_KEEP_SEQS=1 makes
Engine.release keep every slot's sequence as it keeps slot 0's (Engine.keepsSequence); a paged slot still gives its
pages back. The next request on the slot binds the kept sequence, and Engine.begin's State.reset zeroes the KDA conv
windows and states and sets the position and the MTP length, as it does for slot 0 on every request. Off: the same
calls in the same order as before. Memory: the startup plan already holds every admitted stream's arena
(native/cuda.zig: held = stream_bytes * (streams - free)), so a kept sequence never exceeds what the boot admitted;
the bytes stay mapped between requests instead of returning to the host.

Bits: a kept sequence differs from a fresh one only in the bytes State.reset leaves, which every read covers before
it reads them: the dense chunk programs load keys only through pos + r and zero-fill the rest (common/mla.cuh
chunks_wide, glm_glue_attn_ovn.cuh chunks: cp16(..., ok), -INFINITY scores, p = 0); a sparse row's tokens come from
its visible pools only (select_row's limit, glm_glue_index.cuh; tf_glm_index_pools and the split select the same
bound) and dsa_tokens drops tail tokens past the row; a row with no more than 512 complete pools takes the dense path
(counts 0); pool_keys reads ring rows the sequence wrote; the MTP caches the same at mtp_len. Slot 0 relies on this
for every request today, and upstream's TF_KV_POISON receipts serve pages NaN before use with equal replies. The GPU
check glm-keep-seqs-check shows it: a kept sequence, as a 6,000-token prompt left it and with every arena byte
poisoned to 0xFF, gives a fresh one's tokens, bf16 logits rows and MTP drafts for 24 greedy steps after 600- and
3,000-token prompts, bf16 and fp8 caches, byte for byte; the planted control (KDA states written after the reset) differs.

Why (lane RequestStartCost, work/RequestStartCost/): at --parallel 4 the recipe's four-stream decode falls with
--context, 109.73 / 106.98 / 101.95 tok/s at 262,144 / 524,288 / 1,048,576 (cx-* cells, mean of each cell's rounds,
replies equal). Rank 0's captures (~/nsys/ctx256k.sqlite, ctx1024k.sqlite) put three of each wave's four requests on
a made slot: cuMemAlloc 48.2 / 176.1 ms, the fill 10.1 / 38.0 ms (1,907 / 7,127 MiB), the gate's wait for the other
ranks 63.6 / 216.0 ms, a start of 122.5 / 430.8 ms, and a cuMemFree of 16.1 / 59.6 ms at its end (392 ns a window row
for the start, 55 ns for the free); a request on slot 0 starts in 1.5 ms. The three starts and frees are 0.41 / 1.47 s
of a 14.45 / 15.57 s decode window and 94% of its growth from 262,144 to 1,048,576. Expected with the knob: the second
to fourth requests' first tokens 0.25 to 1.35 s sooner at 1M, and four-stream decode near 113 tok/s at every context.

